/technical-due-diligence/"], "citations": [{"title": "HTI-1 final rule general overview fact sheet", "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/sites/default/files/page/2023-12/HTI-1_Gen-Overview_factsheet_508.pdf", "publisher": "ONC, HealthIT.gov", "date": "Dec 2023"}, {"title": "Unveiling progress: analysing the evolution from the HTI-1 proposed rule to final rule", "url": "https://www.drummondgroup.com/blog/analyzing-hti-1-final-rule/", "publisher": "Drummond Group"}, {"title": "HTI-5 proposed rule overview", "url": "https://healthit.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/HTI-5-Proposed-Rule-Overview.pdf", "publisher": "ASTP/ONC, HealthIT.gov", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "HTI-5 proposed rule chart", "url": "https://healthit.gov/resources/hti-5-proposed-rule-chart/", "publisher": "ASTP/ONC, HealthIT.gov"}, {"title": "Federal regulatory update: HHS proposes rule to deregulate health IT and advance AI interoperability (HTI-5)", "url": "https://www.nelsonmullins.com/insights/blogs/healthcare_essentials/data_privacy_and_security/federal-regulatory-update-hhs-proposes-rule-to-deregulate-health-it-and-advance-ai-interoperability-hti-5", "publisher": "Nelson Mullins", "date": "24 Dec 2025"}, {"title": "ASTP/ONC's year-end moves mark a strategic pivot in federal health IT policy", "url": "https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/01/astp-oncs-year-end-moves-mark-a-strategic-pivot", "publisher": "Holland & Knight", "date": "7 Jan 2026"}]}-->
<p>US health IT certification rules shape what millions of clinicians see in their EHRs. In late 2023 the HTI-1 final rule made the most significant AI-related changes yet: transparency duties for predictive decision support. In late 2025 the HTI-5 proposed rule set out to remove many certification requirements, including those AI duties. Health tech leaders are understandably asking what they actually need to build.</p>
<p>This is an architect's reading. Confirm the current status of each rule and how it applies to you with regulatory counsel.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What HTI-1 did</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>USCDI v3 as the baseline.</strong> HTI-1 adopted the United States Core Data for Interoperability version 3 as the baseline standard from 1 January 2026, replacing version 1<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Decision support interventions.</strong> It established a decision support interventions (DSI) criterion and added a <strong>predictive DSI</strong> category covering AI and machine learning capabilities<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Source attributes.</strong> Developers supplying predictive DSIs as part of certified health IT had to make detailed source attribute information available for review, often described as model card requirements, with an enforcement date of 1 January 2025 for the DSI provisions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What HTI-5 proposes</h2>
<p>In December 2025 ASTP/ONC published HTI-5, a deregulatory proposed rule. Commentary describes it as proposing to remove 34 and revise seven of the 60 certification criteria<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. ASTP/ONC's own materials describe the DSI change as reducing the criterion's scope to remove the AI model card requirements<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Some changes were proposed to take effect on publication of a final rule and others later; sources differ on the details<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Holland & Knight characterised the year-end actions as a strategic pivot in federal health IT policy<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. Check whether HTI-5 has been finalised, and in what form, before changing plans.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Regulation can remove a requirement to publish model information. It can't remove a hospital's need to understand the model it is relying on.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why I'd keep building transparency anyway</h2>
<p>Whatever happens to the certification criterion, the underlying questions don't go away:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Customers ask.</strong> Health systems' AI governance committees often ask vendors what data a model was trained on, how it performs in different populations, and how it is monitored. A clear model description shortens procurement.</li>
<li><strong>Other regimes apply.</strong> Products sold internationally may face the <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-digital-omnibus-roadmap/">EU AI Act</a>, and regulated device software may fall under FDA pathways such as those in <a href="/insights/fda-pccp-ai-medical-devices/">FDA PCCPs</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Safety and liability.</strong> When a model contributes to a harmful outcome, the organisation that can show what it knew about the model, and how it monitored it, is in a far stronger position. See <a href="/insights/ai-incident-postmortems/">AI incident postmortems</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Policy swings.</strong> Requirements removed in one cycle can return in another. Building transparency as a product capability is cheaper than switching it on and off.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd build regardless of the rule</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>A model description per predictive feature</strong>: intended use, training data sources, known limitations, performance overall and by relevant subgroups, update history. Keep it as structured data, not a PDF.</li>
<li><strong>Local validation</strong> before go-live at each customer, because performance varies between sites; federation helps, as in <a href="/insights/federated-health-analytics-ohdsi/">federated health analytics</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Ongoing monitoring</strong> for drift and subgroup performance, with a defined path for clinicians to report problems.</li>
<li><strong>Change control</strong>: every model update versioned, evaluated against a fixed set, and recorded.</li>
<li><strong>Interoperable data foundations</strong>: USCDI-aligned FHIR data, which most predictive features depend on, built with the patterns in <a href="/insights/fhir-bulk-data-export-pipelines/">FHIR Bulk Data pipelines</a> and <a href="/insights/tefca-2026-what-health-tech-should-build/">TEFCA</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>These map neatly to general frameworks too, such as those compared in <a href="/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/">ISO 42001 and the NIST AI RMF</a>, so one body of evidence serves several audiences.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>For health systems</h2>
<p>If you buy rather than build, don't let a relaxed certification requirement relax your procurement. Ask vendors for the same information HTI-1 described, contractually, and require notification of model changes. Your governance committee's questions shouldn't depend on which rule is in force this year.</p>
</section>

